= Cognitive load — Technical =

== Element interactivity ==
Cognitive load theory relates intrinsic load to the number and interaction of elements that must be processed simultaneously relative to learner expertise.

An expert can chunk elements that remain separate for a novice.

== Extraneous processing ==
Split-attention, redundancy, transient information, unnecessary search, and poorly coordinated sources can impose processing demands unrelated to the target schema.

Instructional effects are often expertise-dependent.

== Germane load terminology ==
Earlier CLT formulations separated intrinsic, extraneous, and germane load.

Later treatments often reinterpret germane resources as processing devoted to intrinsic aspects of learning rather than a fully independent load source.

Articles should date the terminology rather than present one taxonomy as timeless.

== Measurement problem ==
Subjective effort scales, dual-task costs, pupil dilation, EEG, and performance are imperfect proxies.

A measure can reflect motivation, arousal, fatigue, or task difficulty as well as cognitive load.

